; ---- EGG-2: the moat under the climb ---------------------------------------------------------
; Talon's addendum §7: "One platforming tower currently has a safe floor below its jumping
; section, so falling is consequence-free -- too easy. Replace that floor with the same standard
; drowns-on-touch water used elsewhere in the level, so falling means landing in water and
; drowning."
;
; The 100 x 0.5 x 100 plate above is now a FRAME. The hole is section-local x -32..18, z -18..13
; -- the fall shadow of everything above the tier-1 approach: the spiral pads span x -28.2..0.0
; and z -11.6..12.2, the ladder ledges z -16.8..-13.2, and the balance beam reaches x +15.75. The
; tier-1 scramble (Mesa1 at z -22.1 through BlockD, and the MesaRamp1 entrance at z -25.7) sits
; entirely NORTH of the hole and stays dry, so the climb still has a start.
;
; The basin bed is at y -3.0. WaterGeometry.WaterY is -0.58, so a body on the bed is 2.42 m under
; the surface against a SubmergedDepthM of 1.15 -- lethal, not merely wet. The matching footprint
; is WaterGeometry.BluePrecisionMoat (world x 63..113, z -18..13, floor -5.0); this file authors
; what you see and stand on, that file authors what drowns you, and neither is a copy of the
; other's numbers by accident -- BubbleTestLayoutTests reads the boxes below and checks them
; against the footprint.
;
; TWO GROUND-LEVEL POSTS NOW STAND IN THE WATER and are given footings below rather than left
; hanging: Tier1_Base/BarkLedge3 (x -28.46, z -15.39, top +0.40) and Tier1_Base/PillarD (x -6.00,
; z -17.32, top +3.00). Both are inside the hole and both had their bases on the deleted plate.
; They are the moat's only dry footholds and that is a fair accident: a fall off the ladder can
; land on a 1.2 x 1.6 ledge, and everywhere else is a swim you cannot finish.
[sub_resource type="BoxMesh" id="BoxMesh_egg2_apron_ns"]
size = Vector3(100, 0.5, 32)

[sub_resource type="BoxShape3D" id="BoxShape3D_egg2_apron_ns"]
size = Vector3(100, 0.5, 32)

